1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Transformers are taking the natural language processing world by storm.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
These incredible models are breaking multiple NLP records and pushing the state of the art.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
They are used in many applications like machine language translation, conversational chatbots,

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and even to power better search engines.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Transformers are the rage and deep learning nowadays, but how do they work?

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Why have they outperformed a previous king of sequence problems like recurrent neural networks, GRUs, and LSTMs?

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
You've probably heard of different famous transformer models like BERT, GPT, and GPT2.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
In this video, we'll focus on the one paper that started it all.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Attention is all you need.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
To understand transformers, we first must understand the attention mechanism.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
To get an intuitive understanding of the attention mechanism, let's start with a fun text generation model

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
that's capable of writing its own sci-fi novel.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
We'll need to prime the model with an arbitrary input and a model will generate the rest.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Okay, let's make the story interesting.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
As aliens entered our planet and began to colonize Earth,

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
a certain group of extraterrestrials began to manipulate our society

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
through their influence of a certain number of the elite of the country

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
to keep an iron grip over the populace.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
By the way, I didn't just make this up.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
This was actually generated by OpenAI's GPT2 transformer model.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Shout out to Huggingface for an awesome interface to play with.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
I'll provide a link in the description.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Okay, so the model is a little dark, but what's interesting is how it works.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:45,000
As the model generates text word by word, it has the ability to reference or attend to words that is relevant to the generated word.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
How the model knows which word to attend to is all learned while trading with back propagation.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
R&Ns are also capable of looking at previous inputs too,

27
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
but the power of the attention mechanism is that it doesn't suffer from short-term memory.

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
R&Ns have a shorter window to reference from, so when the story gets longer,

29
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
R&Ns can't access where generated earlier in the sequence.

30
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:16,000
This is still true for GRUs and LSTMs, although they do have a bigger capacity to achieve longer-term memory,

31
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
therefore having a longer window to reference from.

32
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The attention mechanism, in theory, and given enough compute resources, have an infinite window to reference from,

33
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
therefore being capable of using the entire context of the story while generating the text.

34
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
This power was demonstrated in the paper Attention Is All You Need,

35
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
where the authors introduce a new novel neural network called the Transformers,

36
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
which is an attention-based encoder-decoder type architecture.

37
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
On a high level, the encoder maps an input sequence into an abstract continuous representation

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
that holds all the learned information of that input.

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
The decoder then takes that continuous representation,

40
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
and step-by-step generates a single output while also being fed the previous output.

41
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Let's walk through an example.

42
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The Attention Is All You Need paper applied to Transformer model on a neural machine translation problem.

43
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Our demonstration of the Transformer model would be a conversational chatbot.

44
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The example would take in an input text,

45
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
hi, how are you, and generate the response, I am fine.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Let's break down the mechanics of the network step-by-step.

47
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
The first step is feeding our input into a word embedding layer.

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
A word embedding layer can be thought of as a lookup table to grab a learned vector representation of each word.

49
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Neural networks learn through numbers, so each word maps to a vector with continuous values to represent that word.

50
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Next step is to inject positional information into the embeddings.

51
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Because a Transformer encoder has no recurrence like recurrent known networks,

52
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
we must add information about the positions into the input embeddings.

53
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
This is done using positional encoding.

54
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
The authors came up with a clever trick using sign and cosine functions.

55
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
We won't go into the mathematical details of the positional encodings in this video, but here are the basics.

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
For every odd time step, create a vector using the cosine function.

57
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
For every even time step, create a vector using the sine function.

58
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Then add those vectors to their corresponding embedding vector.

59
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
This successfully gives the network information on the positions of each vector.

60
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
The sine and cosine functions were chosen in tandem because they have linear properties the model can easily learn to attend to.

61
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Now we have the encoder layer.

62
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:59,000
The encoder layer's job is to map all input sequence into an abstract continuous representation that holds the learned information for that entire sequence.

63
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
It contains two sub-modules, multi-headed attention, followed by a fully connected network.

64
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,000
There are also residual connections around each of the two sub-modules, followed by a layer normalization.

65
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
To break this down, let's look at the multi-headed attention module.

66
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Multi-headed attention in the encoder applies a specific attention mechanism called self-attention.

67
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Self-attention allows the model to associate each individual word in the input to other words in the input.

68
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
So in our example, it's possible that our model can learn to associate the word you with how and are.

69
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It's also possible that the model learns that words structured in this pattern are typically a question, so respond appropriately.

70
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:55,000
To achieve self-attention, we feed the input into three distinct fully connected layers to create the query, key, and value vectors.

71
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
What are these vectors exactly?

72
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
I found a good explanation on Stack Exchange stating,

73
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The query, key, and value concept comes from the retrieval system.

74
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
For example, when you type a query to search for some video on YouTube, the search engine will map your query against a set of keys,

75
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:21,000
for example, video title, description, etc., associated with candidate videos in the database, then present you with the best match video.

76
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Let's see how this relates to self-attention.

77
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:31,000
The queries and keys undergoes a dot product matrix multiplication to produce a score matrix.

78
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
The score matrix determines how much focus should a word be put on other words,

79
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,000
so each word will have a score that correspond to other words in the time step.

80
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
The higher the score, the more of the focus.

81
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
This is how queries are mapped to keys.

82
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Then, the scores get scaled down by getting divided by the square root of the dimension of the queries and the keys.

83
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:01,000
This is to allow for more stable gradients as multiplying values can have exploding effects.

84
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Next, you take the softmax of the scaled score to get the attention weights, which gives you probability values between 0 and 1.

85
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
By doing the softmax, the higher scores get heightened and the lower scores are depressed.

86
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
This allows the model to be more confident on which words to attend to.

87
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Then, you take the attention weights and multiply it by your value vector to get an output vector.

88
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:28,000
The higher softmax scores will keep the value of the words the model learned is more important.

89
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
The lower scores will drown out the irrelevant words.

90
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
You feed the output vector into a linear layer to process.

91
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:44,000
To make this a multi-headed attention computation, you need to split the query key and value into n vectors before applying self-attention.

92
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
To split vectors then goes through the same self-attention process individually.

93
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Each self-attention process is called a head.

94
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Each head produces an output vector that gets concatenated into a single vector before going through the final linear layer.

95
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:07,000
In theory, each head would learn something different, therefore giving the encoder model more representation power.

96
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Okay, so that's multi-headed attention.

97
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:16,000
To sum it up, multi-headed attention is a module in a transformer network that computes the attention weights for the input

98
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and produces an output vector with encoded information on how each word should attend to all other words in a sequence.

99
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Next step, the multi-headed attention output vector is added to the original input.

100
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
This is called a residual connection.

101
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,000
The output of the residual connection goes through a layer normalization.

102
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:46,000
The normalized residual output gets fed into a point-wise feed forward network for further processing.

103
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The point-wise feed forward network are a couple of linear layers with a relative activation in between.

104
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:59,000
The output of that is again added to the input of the point-wise feed forward network and further normalized.

105
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
The residual connections helps the network train by allowing gradients to flow through the networks directly.

106
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:12,000
The layer normalizations are used to stabilize the network which results in substantially producing the training time necessary

107
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and a point-wise feed forward layer are used to further process the attention output, potentially giving it a richer representation.

108
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
And that wraps up the encoded layer.

109
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:31,000
All of these operations is for the purpose of encoding the input to a continuous representation with attention information.

110
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:37,000
This will help the decoder focus on the appropriate words in the input during the decoding process.

111
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:47,000
You can stack the encoder n times to further encode the information where each layer has the opportunity to learn different attention representations,

112
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:53,000
therefore potentially boosting the predictive power of the transformer network.

113
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Now we move on to the decoder.

114
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
The decoder's job is to generate text sequences.

115
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
The decoder has similar sublayers as the encoder.

116
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:10,000
It has two multi-headed attention layers, a point-wise feed forward layer, with residual connections and layer normalization after each sublayer.

117
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:17,000
These sublayers behave similarly to layers in the encoder but each multi-headed attention layer has a different job.

118
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:23,000
It's capped off with a linear layer that acts like a classifier and a softmax to get the word probabilities.

119
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
The decoder is auto-regressive.

120
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:33,000
It takes in the list of previous outputs as inputs, as well as the encoder outputs that contains the attention information from the input.

121
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
The decoder stops decoding when it generates an n-token as an output.

122
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Let's walk through the decoding steps.

123
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:48,000
The input goes through an embedding layer and a positional encoding layer to get positional embeddings.

124
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:56,000
The positional embeddings gets fed into the first multi-headed attention layer, which computes the attention score for the decoder's input.

125
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
This multi-headed attention layer operates slightly different.

126
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Since the decoder is auto-regressive and generates the sequence word by word, you need to prevent it from conditioning to future tokens.

127
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:19,000
For example, when computing attention scores on the word am, you should not have access to the word find because that word is a future word that was generated after.

128
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
The word am should only have access to itself and the words before it.

129
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
This is true for all other words where they can only attend to previous words.

130
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
We need a method to prevent computing attention scores for future words.

131
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
This method is called masking.

132
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:40,000
To prevent the decoder from looking at future tokens, you apply a look-ahead mask.

133
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
The mask is added before calculating the softmax and after scaling the scores.

134
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Let's take a look at how this works.

135
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:54,000
The mask is a matrix that's the same size as the attention scores filled with values of zeros and negative infinities.

136
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:02,000
When you add the mask to the scaled attention scores, you get a matrix of scores with the top right triangle filled with negative infinities.

137
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:12,000
The reason for this is once you take the softmax of the masked scores, the negative infinities get zeroed out, leaving a zero attention score for future tokens.

138
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:21,000
As you can see, the attention scores for am have values for itself and all other words before it, but zero for the word find.

139
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
This essentially tells the model to put no focus on those words.

140
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:32,000
This masking is the only difference on how the attention scores are calculated in the first multi-headed attention layer.

141
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:41,000
This layer still have multiple heads that the mask are being applied to before getting concatenated and fed through a linear layer for further processing.

142
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:50,000
The output of the first multi-headed attention is a mask output vector with information on how the model should attend on the decoder's inputs.

143
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
Now on to the second multi-headed attention layer.

144
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,000
For this layer, the encoder's output are the queries and the keys, and the first multi-headed attention layer outputs are the values.

145
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:11,000
This process matches the encoder's input to the decoder's input, allowing the decoder to decide which encoder input is relevant to put focus on.

146
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:17,000
The output of the second multi-headed attention goes through a point-wise feed forward layer for further processing.

147
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
The output of the final point-wise feed forward layer goes through a final linear layer that acts as a classifier.

148
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
The classifier is as big as the number of classes you have.

149
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:36,000
For example, if you have 10,000 classes for 10,000 words, the output of that classifier will be of size 10,000.

150
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
The output of the classifier then gets fed into a softmax layer.

151
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:46,000
The softmax layer produce probability scores between 0 and 1 for each class.

152
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,000
We take the index of the highest probability score and that equals our predicted word.

153
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:59,000
The decoder then takes the output and adds it to the list of decoder inputs and continue decoding again until the end token is predicted.

154
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
For our case, the highest probability prediction is the final class, which is assigned to the end token.

155
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
This is how the decoder generates the output.

156
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:15,000
The decoder can be stacked n layers high, each layer taking in inputs from the encoder and the layers before it.

157
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:22,000
By stacking the layers, the model can learn to extract and focus on different combinations of attention from its attention heads,

158
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
potentially boosting its predictive power.

159
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:29,000
And that's it. That's the mechanics of the transformers.

160
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:34,000
Transformers leverage the power of the attention mechanism to make better predictions.

161
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Recurrent known networks try to achieve similar things, but because they suffer from short-term memory,

162
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,000
transformers are usually better, especially if you want to encode or generate longer sequences.

163
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Because of the transformer architecture, the natural language processing industry can now achieve unprecedented results.

164
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
If you found this helpful, hit that like and subscribe button.

165
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Also, let me know in the comments what you'd like to see next.

166
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And until next time, thanks for watching.

